NNAAMMEE
       shm_overview - overview of POSIX shared memory

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The POSIX shared memory API allows processes to communicate information
       by sharing a region of memory.

       The interfaces employed in the API are:

       sshhmm__ooppeenn(3)    Create  and  open  a new object, or open an existing ob‐
                      ject.  This is analogous to ooppeenn(2).  The call returns a
                      file descriptor for use by the other  interfaces  listed
                      below.

       ffttrruunnccaattee(2)   Set the size of the shared memory object.  (A newly cre‐
                      ated shared memory object has a length of zero.)

       mmmmaapp(2)        Map  the  shared  memory object into the virtual address
                      space of the calling process.

       mmuunnmmaapp(2)      Unmap the shared memory object from the virtual  address
                      space of the calling process.

       sshhmm__uunnlliinnkk(3)  Remove a shared memory object name.

       cclloossee(2)       Close  the file descriptor allocated by sshhmm__ooppeenn(3) when
                      it is no longer needed.

       ffssttaatt(2)       Obtain a _s_t_a_t structure that describes the shared memory
                      object.  Among the information returned by this call are
                      the  object’s  size  (_s_t___s_i_z_e),  permissions  (_s_t___m_o_d_e),
                      owner (_s_t___u_i_d), and group (_s_t___g_i_d).

       ffcchhoowwnn(2)      To change the ownership of a shared memory object.

       ffcchhmmoodd(2)      To change the permissions of a shared memory object.

   VVeerrssiioonnss
       POSIX shared memory is supported since Linux 2.4 and glibc 2.2.

   PPeerrssiisstteennccee
       POSIX  shared  memory  objects have kernel persistence: a shared memory
       object will exist until the system is shut down, or until all processes
       have unmapped the object and it has been deleted with sshhmm__uunnlliinnkk(3)

   LLiinnkkiinngg
       Programs using the POSIX shared memory API must  be  compiled  with  _c_c
       _-_l_r_t to link against the real‐time library, _l_i_b_r_t.

   AAcccceessssiinngg sshhaarreedd mmeemmoorryy oobbjjeeccttss vviiaa tthhee ffiilleessyysstteemm
       On  Linux,  shared  memory  objects are created in a (ttmmppffss(5)) virtual
       filesystem, normally mounted under _/_d_e_v_/_s_h_m.  Since Linux 2.6.19, Linux
       supports the use of access control lists (ACLs) to control the  permis‐
       sions of objects in the virtual filesystem.

NNOOTTEESS
       Typically,  processes  must synchronize their access to a shared memory
       object, using, for example, POSIX semaphores.

       System V shared memory (sshhmmggeett(2), sshhmmoopp(2), etc.) is an  older  shared
       memory  API.   POSIX  shared  memory provides a simpler, and better de‐
       signed interface; on the other hand POSIX  shared  memory  is  somewhat
       less  widely  available  (especially  on  older  systems) than System V
       shared memory.

